Hi Sheena/Alex,

I reviewed the Steel Forecasting planning functionality and wanted to clarify the process that needs to be followed after the planning dates are entered.
The functionality is working as expected. The main point is that entering the Wall Start and Wall End dates alone does not generate the forecast. The forecast needs to be built afterward.
 

Planning and Build Process
1. Project Planning
Enter or update the Wall Start and Wall End dates under Project Planning.
These dates provide the planning information that will be used for the structure forecast.
2. Structure Planning – Build
After the dates are entered:
Open the Structure Planning tab.
Select the applicable structure.
Click Build.
 
The Build function generates the monthly forecast for that structure.
The system determines the forecast period based on the available planning dates. For the Wall Start/End dates, the steel forecast is planned one month ahead of the wall schedule.
It then distributes the structure's total steel tons across the applicable forecast months.
For example, if the wall schedule falls in October and November, the corresponding steel planning will be generated for September and October.

3. Product Planning – Build
Once the structure forecasts have been generated:
Open the Product Planning tab.
Click Build.
 

The Product Planning Build takes the forecasts generated at the structure level and consolidates them by month for the complete product.

Therefore, the order is important:
Project Planning → Structure Planning Build → Product Planning Build → Steel Forecasting Report

Excel Planning Option
There is also an Excel Planning option available on all three planning tabs:
Project Planning
Structure Planning
Product Planning
If the user wants to maintain the planning information through Excel, they can click the Excel Planning button on the applicable tab.
The process is:
Click Excel Planning.
Download the Excel planning sheet.
Update the required planning information in Excel.
Save the updated file.
Upload the Excel sheet back into the application.
I have also tested the Excel Planning download/update/upload functionality, and it is working as expected.
So users have both options available: they can maintain the planning information directly through the application or use the Excel Planning functionality for bulk/easier updates.
For the issue we reviewed, the Wall Start/End dates had been entered, but the subsequent forecast build steps had not been completed. Once the Structure Planning Build and then the Product Planning Build were performed, the forecast was generated correctly. Once the Structure Forecast and Product Forecast have been created, the Steel Forecasting Power BI report should reflect the updated forecast data after its next scheduled refresh.

